Discovery failure transaction and diagnostic boundaries
======================================================

A generic discovery error first rolls back the current inventory transaction.
Failure status must not commit pending device updates or inserts. If an earlier
phase already committed inventory, rollback does not undo that completed phase.
The failure result therefore does not claim that all external work was undone.

Before recording controller ERROR, the handler reloads and locks the current
controller, site and organization, requiring the original site and tenant and
active, undeleted authority. A moved/disabled/deleted owner is refused without
modifying its status or publishing a failure event attributed to that owner.
Current configuration is loaded again, preserving unrelated concurrent edits.
The authority locks remain held through the error-record commit.

New error fields, the failure event, task return and this handler's log use
fixed messages. Exception text, SQL parameters and vendor response bodies are
not copied. When rewriting error history, only up to nine previous valid,
bounded timestamps are retained with a fixed historical label; arbitrary
legacy bodies/extra fields are discarded. Unrelated configuration remains.
This is not a global migration or erasure of old diagnostic storage. Controllers
which never enter this handler retain their prior records until a separate
migration or administrative retention process addresses them.

A bookkeeping/database failure leaves the task unsuccessful and logs a fixed
category. A failed event publication after the error-record commit also retains
a failed outcome; it must not trigger automatic repetition of discovery effects.
The event is still best effort, not a transactional outbox. Pre-commit device
events can already have escaped before rollback, and previously committed deep
sync/ZTP/hardware actions are not transactionally reversed. Success-path partial
errors, per-controller retry diagnostics, native Celery logs/results, execution-
time caller grants and postcommit authorization races remain separate work.

PostgreSQL checks cover updates, savepoint inserts, aborted outer transactions,
current authority changes and locks, malformed/legacy histories, event outages,
and successful/previously committed inventory controls. Separate application
processes execute the real transaction path with synthetic adapters, deep sync,
ZTP and event transport. No real hardware or external notification is contacted.
